WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.000
Every meme is in the same typeface.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.649
It’s called Impact.

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:05.569
But how did that happen?

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.120
Impact was designed by Geoffrey Lee in 1963.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.809
It was bold, condensed &amp; sans-serif, in line
with the style of the 60s

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:17.630
and ideal for short pieces of text and punchy
headlines.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.650
Impact was influenced by a movement called
the International Typographic Style or Swiss

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.650
Style,

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:29.100
which emphasized readability and cleanliness,
with typefaces like Helvetica and Univers.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.300
It took those staid fonts and gave them a
dash of mod flavor.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.750
Those were the days of metal typesetting,
so Lee had to carve each letter by hand.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.360
Lee sold it to the type manufacturer Stephenson
Blake.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.740
This is their original ad for the font.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.220
Impact was later sold to Monotype, another
typeface company -- they’re known for creating

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.640
Times New Roman.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.920
And Monotype in turn licensed fonts to Microsoft.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.470
So that’s how Impact ended up being included
in the Windows Operating System

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.220
which was starting to dominate the market
in the 90s.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.499
And in 1996, Microsoft chose 11 “core fonts
for the web”

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:11.439
a free, standard font pack that would work
across the internet.

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:12.969
Impact was one of them.

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:15.070
By the way, that’s Webdings.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.439
That distribution advantage helped Impact
become the default in online forums.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:22.539
Those memes were called ‘image macros’
after ‘macro instructions’

00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:27.890
a term in computer science for a line of code
that requests a pre-defined sequence of instructions.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.990
And in 2003, one of these image macros — a
picture of a cat with the words

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.299
“I can haz cheezburger” — launched the
wave of modern memes.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:42.700
Several meme-generator sites subsequently
adopted Impact as well, adding a black outline

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.340
on the white font.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.569
There are more than 11 fonts on the internet
now.

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:52.090
But we expect memes to look a certain way,
and that includes Impact.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:02:08.280
And that’s probably not going to change
anytime soon.

